### What is Apache Flink?

Apache Flink is an open-source, distributed stream-processing engine designed for stateful computations over unbounded and bounded data streams. Its core model is the dataflow graph: your program is a directed acyclic graph of operators (sources, transformations, sinks) through which events flow continuously. Every event is processed as it arrives, so latency can be in the low milliseconds.

```java
package com.bank.streaming;

import org.apache.flink.streaming.api.environment.StreamExecutionEnvironment;
import org.apache.flink.streaming.api.datastream.DataStream;

public class FlinkIntroDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        StreamExecutionEnvironment env =
            StreamExecutionEnvironment.getExecutionEnvironment();

        DataStream<String> transactions = env.socketTextStream("localhost", 9092);
        transactions
            .filter(txn -> txn.contains("FRAUD"))
            .print("Suspicious");

        env.execute("NBI Fraud Alert Demo");
    }
}
```

```text
Suspicious> FRAUD:ACC-10045:Rs5000:Mumbai
Suspicious> FRAUD:ACC-10045:Rs8000:Delhi
```

### Why does Apache Flink exist?

Before Flink, real-time stream processing relied on micro-batch engines like Spark Structured Streaming or on low-level frameworks like Storm that lacked state management. Flink was created to give developers a true event-at-a-time engine with built-in state, event-time semantics, and exactly-once guarantees. For a bank, this means fraud detection, live risk limits, and instant settlement signals can run with sub-second latency and correct results even when events arrive late or out of order.

```java
package com.bank.streaming;

import org.apache.flink.streaming.api.environment.StreamExecutionEnvironment;
import org.apache.flink.streaming.api.windowing.time.Time;
import org.apache.flink.streaming.api.windowing.assigners.TumblingEventTimeWindows;

public class WhyFlinkDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        StreamExecutionEnvironment env =
            StreamExecutionEnvironment.getExecutionEnvironment();

        // Event-time window: correct even if events arrive late
        env.fromElements(
                new Transaction("ACC-001", 5000.00, 1700000000000L),
                new Transaction("ACC-001", 3000.00, 1700000010000L))
            .assignTimestampsAndWatermarks(
                WatermarkStrategy.<Transaction>forBoundedOutOfOrderness(
                    Duration.ofSeconds(5))
                .withTimestampAssigner((txn, ts) -> txn.getEventTime()))
            .keyBy(Transaction::getAccountId)
            .window(TumblingEventTimeWindows.of(Time.minutes(1)))
            .sum("amount")
            .print("Per-minute total");

        env.execute("NBI Event-Time Demo");
    }
}
```

### How does Flink process data internally?

Flink's runtime is built on a pipelined dataflow execution model. The JobManager splits the user program into an execution graph of parallel subtasks. Each subtask runs inside a task slot on a TaskManager. Records flow through the graph continuously via network buffers. When a shuffle is needed (for example after a `keyBy`), records are serialized, sent over the network to the correct downstream subtask, and deserialized. When operators can be chained (same parallelism, no shuffle), Flink fuses them into a single thread, eliminating serialization overhead.

```java
package com.bank.streaming;

import org.apache.flink.streaming.api.environment.StreamExecutionEnvironment;

public class InternalProcessingDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        StreamExecutionEnvironment env =
            StreamExecutionEnvironment.getExecutionEnvironment();
        env.setParallelism(4); // 4 subtasks per operator

        env.fromElements(
                "ACC-001:5000", "ACC-002:3000", "ACC-001:2000", "ACC-003:7000")
            .map(line -> {
                String[] parts = line.split(":");
                return new Transaction(parts[0],
                    Double.parseDouble(parts[1]), System.currentTimeMillis());
            })
            .keyBy(Transaction::getAccountId) // shuffle by account
            .sum("amount")
            .print("Running total");

        env.execute("NBI Internal Processing Demo");
    }
}
```

```text
Running total> Transaction{accountId=ACC-001, amount=5000.0}
Running total> Transaction{accountId=ACC-002, amount=3000.0}
Running total> Transaction{accountId=ACC-001, amount=7000.0}
Running total> Transaction{accountId=ACC-003, amount=7000.0}
```

### How is Flink different from Apache Spark Structured Streaming?

| Feature | Apache Flink | Spark Structured Streaming |
|---|---|---|
| Processing model | True event-at-a-time | Micro-batch (continuous mode experimental) |
| Latency | Low milliseconds | Hundreds of milliseconds to seconds |
| State management | First-class keyed state with RocksDB | State store with watermark, less flexible |
| Event-time handling | Native watermarks, allowed lateness, side outputs | Watermark support, less granular late handling |
| Exactly-once | Barrier-based checkpoints + 2PC sinks | Micro-batch transaction per batch |
| Batch mode | Bounded stream (special case) | Native batch engine |
| API | DataStream, Table API, Flink SQL | DataFrame, Structured Streaming API |

```java
// Flink: true event-at-a-time with sub-second latency
package com.bank.streaming;

import org.apache.flink.streaming.api.environment.StreamExecutionEnvironment;

public class FlinkVsSparkDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        StreamExecutionEnvironment env =
            StreamExecutionEnvironment.getExecutionEnvironment();

        // Each transaction processed instantly, no micro-batch delay
        env.addSource(new KafkaSource<>("nbi-transactions"))
            .keyBy(Transaction::getAccountId)
            .process(new FraudScoringFunction()) // per-event, stateful
            .addSink(new AlertSink());

        env.execute("NBI Real-Time Fraud Scoring");
    }
}
```

### What are the core components of a Flink cluster?

A Flink cluster has two main processes. The JobManager is the coordinator: it receives the job graph, schedules tasks across TaskManagers, triggers checkpoints, and handles failover. The TaskManager is the worker: it runs the actual subtasks inside task slots, manages local state, and exchanges data over the network. A ResourceManager handles resource allocation depending on the deployment mode (YARN, Kubernetes, or standalone).

```java
// Configuration example for a Flink cluster
// flink-conf.yaml
// jobmanager.rpc.address: jobmanager-host
// taskmanager.numberOfTaskSlots: 4
// taskmanager.memory.process.size: 4096m
// state.backend: rocksdb
// state.checkpoints.dir: s3://nbi-checkpoints/flink/
```

```text
Cluster layout:
  JobManager (1 instance)
    - Receives job graph
    - Schedules subtasks
    - Triggers checkpoints

  TaskManager (N instances, each with M slots)
    - Runs subtasks
    - Manages local state
    - Exchanges data via network buffers
```

### What are the state backends in Flink?

Flink provides two production state backends. The HashMapStateBackend keeps state as Java objects on the JVM heap, giving fast access but limited by heap size. The EmbeddedRocksDBStateBackend stores state in RocksDB on local disk with a block cache in managed off-heap memory, supporting state much larger than memory at the cost of serialization overhead on every access. Both backends checkpoint to a durable store such as S3 or HDFS.

```java
package com.bank.streaming;

import org.apache.flink.contrib.streaming.state.EmbeddedRocksDBStateBackend;
import org.apache.flink.streaming.api.environment.StreamExecutionEnvironment;

public class StateBackendDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        StreamExecutionEnvironment env =
            StreamExecutionEnvironment.getExecutionEnvironment();

        // Use RocksDB for large per-account state
        env.setStateBackend(new EmbeddedRocksDBStateBackend(true));
        env.getCheckpointConfig().setCheckpointStorage(
            "s3://nbi-checkpoints/flink/");

        env.addSource(new KafkaSource<>("nbi-transactions"))
            .keyBy(Transaction::getAccountId)
            .process(new AccountAggregator()) // months of per-account state
            .addSink(new LedgerSink());

        env.execute("NBI Stateful Aggregation");
    }
}
```

### What are the rules and constraints of Flink?

Flink enforces several rules that developers must understand. Parallelism cannot exceed the total number of task slots available. Keyed state is scoped to the current key and cannot be accessed across keys. Watermarks must advance monotonically within a source partition. Checkpoints require replayable sources (like Kafka) for exactly-once recovery. State classes used with the RocksDB backend must be serializable by Flink's type system.

```java
package com.bank.streaming;

import org.apache.flink.api.common.state.ValueState;
import org.apache.flink.api.common.state.ValueStateDescriptor;
import org.apache.flink.streaming.api.functions.KeyedProcessFunction;
import org.apache.flink.util.Collector;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class StateRulesDemo
        extends KeyedProcessFunction<String, Transaction, String> {

    // State is scoped to the current key (account)
    private transient ValueState<BigDecimal> runningTotal;

    @Override
    public void open(Configuration parameters) {
        runningTotal = getRuntimeContext().getState(
            new ValueStateDescriptor<>("runningTotal", BigDecimal.class));
    }

    @Override
    public void processElement(Transaction txn, Context ctx,
            Collector<String> out) throws Exception {
        BigDecimal current = runningTotal.value();
        if (current == null) current = BigDecimal.ZERO;
        BigDecimal updated = current.add(txn.getAmount());
        runningTotal.update(updated);
        out.collect("Account " + ctx.getCurrentKey()
            + " running total: Rs" + updated);
    }
}
```

### What are common mistakes when starting with Flink?

The most common mistake is using processing time when event time is needed, which gives non-deterministic results that break reconciliation. Another mistake is not configuring a state backend for production, leaving the default heap backend that cannot handle large state. Forgetting to set checkpoint storage means no recovery on failure. Using blocking I/O calls inside operators kills throughput.

```java
// WRONG: using processing time for financial aggregation
stream
    .keyBy(Transaction::getAccountId)
    .window(TumblingProcessingTimeWindows.of(Time.minutes(1)))
    .sum("amount"); // non-deterministic: replay gives different results

// CORRECT: using event time with watermarks
stream
    .assignTimestampsAndWatermarks(
        WatermarkStrategy.<Transaction>forBoundedOutOfOrderness(
            Duration.ofSeconds(5))
        .withTimestampAssigner((txn, ts) -> txn.getEventTime()))
    .keyBy(Transaction::getAccountId)
    .window(TumblingEventTimeWindows.of(Time.minutes(1)))
    .sum("amount"); // deterministic: same data always gives same result
```

### When should you use Flink?

Use Flink when you need real-time processing with sub-second latency, stateful computations over streams (fraud detection, running totals, session analysis), event-time correctness with out-of-order data, and exactly-once guarantees. Banking use cases include real-time fraud scoring, live risk limit enforcement, instant settlement signals, continuous regulatory reporting, and real-time customer dashboards.

```java
package com.bank.fraud;

import org.apache.flink.streaming.api.environment.StreamExecutionEnvironment;

public class FraudPipelineDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        StreamExecutionEnvironment env =
            StreamExecutionEnvironment.getExecutionEnvironment();
        env.enableCheckpointing(60_000); // checkpoint every minute

        env.addSource(new KafkaSource<>("nbi-card-swipes"))
            .assignTimestampsAndWatermarks(nbiWatermarkStrategy())
            .keyBy(CardSwipe::getAccountId)
            .process(new FraudRuleEngine()) // stateful, timer-driven
            .addSink(new AlertService());

        env.execute("NBI Real-Time Fraud Pipeline");
    }
}
```

### When should you NOT use Flink?

Flink is not the right choice for simple batch ETL where latency does not matter and Spark or a warehouse query engine is simpler. It is overkill for low-volume event processing where a serverless function (AWS Lambda) suffices. It adds unnecessary complexity for request-response microservice patterns where a REST API is the natural fit.

```text
Decision guide:
  Need sub-second latency?           --> YES: consider Flink
  Need stateful stream processing?    --> YES: Flink is ideal
  Need event-time correctness?        --> YES: Flink with watermarks
  Simple daily batch ETL?             --> NO: use Spark or warehouse SQL
  Low volume, few events per second?  --> NO: use Lambda or a simple consumer
  Request-response pattern?           --> NO: use a REST microservice
```

### What should a senior engineer know about Flink?

A senior engineer should understand the interaction between checkpointing interval and end-to-end latency, how RocksDB state backend performance depends on managed memory sizing, the difference between aligned and unaligned checkpoints under backpressure, savepoint compatibility and state schema evolution, and how to monitor a production job using Flink metrics exported to Prometheus and Grafana.

```java
package com.bank.streaming;

import org.apache.flink.streaming.api.environment.StreamExecutionEnvironment;
import org.apache.flink.streaming.api.environment.CheckpointConfig;

public class SeniorConfigDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        StreamExecutionEnvironment env =
            StreamExecutionEnvironment.getExecutionEnvironment();

        // Senior-level checkpoint tuning
        env.enableCheckpointing(30_000); // 30s interval
        CheckpointConfig cfg = env.getCheckpointConfig();
        cfg.setMinPauseBetweenCheckpoints(10_000);
        cfg.setCheckpointTimeout(120_000);
        cfg.enableUnalignedCheckpoints(); // for backpressure tolerance
        cfg.setExternalizedCheckpointCleanup(
            CheckpointConfig.ExternalizedCheckpointCleanup
                .RETAIN_ON_CANCELLATION);

        env.execute("NBI Production Config Demo");
    }
}
```
